  "prompt_text": "This figure presents a broad landscape view of how wildfire affects climate, mountains, soils, water, and ecosystem recovery. A large smoke plume from an active fire carries greenhouse gases and pyrogenic carbon while also increasing aerosols that reduce incoming solar radiation. In the mountains, soot deposition darkens snow, and localized rainfall over burned slopes appears to trigger erosion, debris flows, and changes in topography.\n\nAcross the burned hillsides, the image emphasizes cascading post-fire effects: fractured and exposed soil, altered soil color and albedo, and strong soil erosion that transports ash and sediment downslope into streams and lakes. The aquatic system then receives nitrogen, phosphorus, and sediment, which are linked to algal blooms and post-fire flooding. Permafrost thaw or loss is also shown near the stream, suggesting that fire can destabilize cold-region terrain.\n\nAt the same time, the figure highlights biological and human responses. Vegetation recovery is shown through resprouting grasses and tree resprouting from stems or bases, framed as evolutionary adaptations. Wildlife interactions such as pyric herbivory are included, as is Indigenous burning, indicating intentional fire use within the broader fire-affected landscape. Overall, the image depicts wildfire as a connected Earth-system process with both damaging and regenerative outcomes.",
